.\n\n- Play F2: F2 is part of a pair. Discarding F2 would reduce the number of pairs. But maybe if I can form another pair, it's okay.\n\nThe key here is to decide which tile to discard to maximize the chances of forming seven pairs. Since I have three pairs already (W4, B3, F2), I need four more pairs. The remaining tiles are W5, W7, T2, T8, T9, B1, B5, B8. Each of these is a single, so I need to draw one more of each to form pairs. But with 11 tiles, I need to discard one and then draw one. So, if I discard a tile that's not part of a pair, maybe I can form a pair on another tile.\n\nFor example, if I discard B5, which is a single, then I have 10 tiles left. Then, if I draw another B5, I can form a pair. But if I don't, I can still try to form pairs on other tiles. Alternatively, if I discard a different tile, like W5, maybe I can form a pair on W4 (since I have two W4s) if I draw a third W4. But that's a Tile Hog, which is 2 points. However, Tile Hog is only 2 points, and I need at least 8. So maybe not the way to go.\n\nWait, the Seven Pairs pattern is worth 24 points, which is sufficient. So if I can form seven pairs, that's a winning hand. Let's see: I have three pairs already. If I can form four more pairs from the remaining tiles. The remaining tiles are W5, W7, T2, T8, T9, B1, B5, B8. Each of these is a single. To form four more pairs, I need to have four pairs from these eight tiles. That's possible if I can pair two of them. For example, if I draw a T2, then I can have T2 x2. Similarly for T8, T9, etc. But with only one of each, it's unlikely unless I draw the same tile again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can discard one of these singles to make it easier to form pairs. For example, if I discard B5, then I have seven more tiles to form four pairs. Let's see: W5, W7, T2, T8, T9, B1, B8. If I can pair two of these, plus the existing pairs. But it's still challenging.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should focus on keeping the pairs I have. The pairs are W4, B3, F2. 