 to form that chow.\n\nDiscarding T2: T2 is a single. Maybe part of a T1-T2-T3 chow. But the player across has a W1-W2-W3 chow. Wait, the player across has a meld of W1-W2-W3. So they have that chow. So if I have T2, maybe it's part of a T2-T3-T4 chow. But I have T4 in my hand. If I can get a T3, which has 4 left, then T2-T3-T4. So T2 is useful for that. Discarding T2 would lose that possibility.\n\nNow, considering the valid actions, which tile is the safest to discard? The one that is least likely to be used by others and that I can do without.\n\nIf I discard W5, I lose a tile that could be part of a chow with W4 and W6. But if I keep it, maybe I can form that chow. However, if someone else needs W5, they might take it. But if I discard W5, it's possible they take it. But if I discard T4, which is part of a potential T2-T4-T6 chow? Not sure. Alternatively, T2-T4-T6 isn't a valid chow since they're not consecutive. T2-T3-T4 would be consecutive. So T4 could be part of that if I have T2 and T3. But I don't have T3.\n\nLooking at my hand, I have T2 and T4. To make a chow, I need T3. Since T3 has 4 left, it's possible. So keeping T2 and T4 might be useful if I can get T3. So discarding T4 would be bad because it's part of that potential chow.\n\nSimilarly, T8 and T9 could be part of a T7-T8-T9 chow if I get T7. So discarding either of those would be risky if they can be part of a chow.\n\nB6 is part of B5-B6-B7 chow. Since the next player has B5 pung, if I discard B6, they might take it for a chow. But B7 has 3 left, so possible. Alternatively, keeping B6 for that.\n\nDiscarding W5 might be a good option because it's a middle tile that could be part of a chow, but if I discard it, others might take it. However, if I keep it, I can form a chow with W4 and W6. Let's see, if I have two W4s and one W5, and if I can get a W6, that would be a chow. So keeping W5 is better for that.\n\nWhat about the melds I have? I have B3 pung and F2 pung. So I need two more melds and a p