5, keeping two W4s, that's better for forming a Pung of W4. Alternatively, playing B6, keeping one, to form a Pung of B6. \n\nWhich is more likely? The unshown tiles have 4 W4s and 4 B6s. So both are available. But since I have two W4s already, if I play one, there's a chance someone might take it for a Chi. But if I play W5, someone might take it for a Chow. \n\nAlternatively, if I play T2, T8, or T9, those are single tiles and might not be part of any melds I can form. \n\nSo between playing W5, W4, B6. \n\nIf I play W5, I can keep two W4s. If I later draw a W4, that's a Pung. Then I need another B6. \n\nIf I play B6, I keep one B6. If I draw another B6, that's a Pung. Then I need a W4. \n\nWhich is more probable? Well, the number of remaining tiles is the same for both. But since I have two W4s already, maybe it's better to focus on forming a Pung of W4 first. \n\nBut I also have two B6s. Maybe forming a Pung of B6 is easier. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another pattern. For example, if I can form a Pure Straight with W4, W5, W6 and others. But I don't have W6. \n\nAnother angle: the current hand has two Pungs (B3 and F2), and if I can add two more Pungs (W4 and B6), that would give me four Pungs, which is 8 points. That's the All Pungs pattern. \n\nSo to reach four Pungs, I need two more. So playing a tile that allows me to form those two Pungs. \n\nIf I play W5, then I have two W4s. If I get another W4, that's a Pung. Then I need a third B6. \n\nIf I play B6, then I have one B6. If I get another B6, that's a Pung. Then I need a third W4. \n\nWhich is better? Since I have two W4s already, and two B6s, maybe playing one of the B6s is better because I can form a Pung with just one more B6. Whereas for W4, I need one more. \n\nWait, no. If I have two B6s and play one, I have one left. To make a Pung, I need two more. Whereas if I have two W4s and play one, I have one left. To make a Pung, I need two more. So actually, both require two more tiles. \n\nHmm. Maybe the key is which tile is less likely to be taken by others. For example, if I play W5, someone might take it for a Chow. If I play B6, maybe less likely. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, B6 has 4 remaining. Since I have two, there are two more in the wall. If I play one, there are three left. \n\nW5 has 4 remaining. If I play it, there are three left. \n\nSo maybe it's similar. \n\nAlternatively, considering the current melds. The next player has B5 and B8 kongs. So B6 is in between. If someone has a B5 and B6, they might take a B6 for a Chow. But since B5 is already a kong, maybe they don't need it. \n\nAlternatively, if I play W5, someone with W4 and W6 could take it. But since the unshown W4 has 4 remaining, maybe it's less likely. \n\nI think the safer play is to play W5. Because if I play W5, it's a middle tile that might be taken for a Chow, but I still have two W4s to form a Pung. Alternatively, if I play B6, it's a single tile that might be taken for a Pung if someone has two B6s. \n\nBut since I have two B6s, playing one might not be as risky. \n\nWait, but if I play B6, and someone has a Pung of B6, they might take it. However, since there are four B6s in total, and I have two, there are two left. If someone has a Pung, they would have three, but since there are only four in total, it's possible. But if I play B6, and someone has two B6s, they could take it to form a Pung. \n\nAlternatively, if I play W5, someone with W4 and W6 could take it for a Chow. \n\nSo which is more likely? \n\nGiven that the next player has B5 and B8 kongs, maybe they are focusing on those. The player across has a Chi on W2, which might mean they have some W tiles. The previous player has a Peng on F1. \n\nNot sure. \n\nAnother angle: the hand tiles. If I play W5, my hand becomes [W4, W4, T2, T8, T9, B6, B6]. Then I need to form two more Pungs. If I get a W4 and a B6, that would work. \n\nIf I play B6, my hand becomes [W4, W4, W5, T2, T8, T9, B6]. Then I need to get two more B6s