ht be part of a chow for others.\n\nBut looking at the unshown tiles, W4 has 3 copies left. If I discard W4, someone might take it for a chow, but I don't have the other tiles to form a chow. Similarly for T2.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T3, I lose the pair, but maybe that's necessary if I can form a different hand. However, without the pair, it's harder to win.\n\nGiven the options, I think the best move is to discard W4, as it's a single tile and not part of any possible meld for me. That way, I keep the T3 pair and the other tiles that might be part of future melds.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding W4 to maintain potential for forming a pair of T3 and improve chances of completing a winning hand with additional draws.]\nReason: [Current hand lacks sufficient melds. Discarding W4 (a single tile) preserves the T3 pair and keeps other tiles (W7, W8, T2, T8, T9) open for potential chows or pungs. The two pungs (B3 and F2) already contribute 2 points (Mixed Double Pung), and a concealed hand (Fully Concealed) adds 4 points. Discarding W4 maximizes flexibility for future draws to complete the required melds.]\nAnswer: Play W4"}
